WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:07.050
Traditional machine learning approaches have
been difficult to apply to genetics problems

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:11.650
and problems in genomics because these traditional
approaches have often assumed really really

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.990
large sample sizes which we simply don't have.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:18.710
There are a huge number of variables that
we measure for every single individual, for

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:23.490
example, all the mutations in your genotype,
all of the different gene expression levels

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:29.480
for individual cell types, individual tissues,
all the different phenotypes, so every single

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.880
disease you may or may not be diagnosed with,
and all of these different features have really

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.410
complex structures.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.250
And while machine learning methods are incredibly
good at modeling those structures, they require

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.800
a huge amount of data, a huge amount of independent
samples to be able to get really nice signal

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.220
back from them.We simply don't have that in
genomics.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.970
My group has been developing a lot of great
approaches to try and add additional structure

00:00:53.970 --> 00:01:00.020
to these models to be able to exploit what
structure there is in these complex highly

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:05.150
correlated features that we have like gene
expression and like genotype data in order

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:10.390
to exploit the few samples that we have as
much as possible.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.609
Another big difference is actually labeled
data.

00:01:12.609 --> 00:01:18.020
So a lot of traditional machine learning approaches
use supervised data, in other words they have

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.219
ground truth, they know the truth about what's
the underlying signals so if you think about

00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:28.490
emails we often have a bunch of emails that
have either a spam or a not spam label on

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:29.490
them.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.280
And we can learn predictive models for future
emails.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.170
But we also don't have these high quality
labeled data sets in genomics, we simply don't

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:43.579
know exactly which mutation causes most diseases,
we don't know which mutation affects gene

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:46.049
expression levels in particular tissues.

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:51.200
We know maybe the range, we know maybe a large
set of them, we don't know the exact one,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.859
and that really causes us to need to think
about different types of models for the genomic

00:01:56.859 --> 00:01:59.569
data sets that have no ground truth to them.

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:03.490
My group generally thinks about unsupervised
problems, so we're more interested in thinking

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.069
about different patterns that we find in the
data as opposed to doing predictive models.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:12.430
So we're interested in thinking about very,
very small signals, very small patterns that

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:17.500
we find in the data that may indicate, for
example propensity for disease, so looking

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:23.090
at small changes in gene expression levels,
correlated gene expression levels that may

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:29.390
be indicative for example of thyroid cancer,
or of Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:34.460
The key about these types of models is that
there is a lot of differences in each of the

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:39.150
individuals in the world, every single person
has a lot of small patterns that we could

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:44.790
find but really we're interested in finding
the ones that are indicative of disease.

